Glob implementation provenance and licenses

Traversal is adapted from Node.js v22.14.0 lib/internal/fs/glob.js:
https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.14.0/lib/internal/fs/glob.js
The local traversal uses one I/O-request generator with sync/async drivers,
the existing filesystem bindings, and genuine public Dirent objects.

Node.js v22.14.0 bundles minimatch 10.0.1:
https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.14.0/deps/minimatch/package.json
This port deliberately vendors minimatch 11.0.4 for its upstream nonrecursive
matching engine and bounded matcher cache, rather than retaining recursive
matching or maintaining a second custom parser. The compatibility target stays
Node.js v22.14.0. Runtime controls pin both path dialects, brace ranges/nesting,
nested extglobs, POSIX classes, separators, dot/parent segments and drive/UNC roots.

The traversal retains Node.js v22.14.0's asymmetric read-error policy: sync
readdir failures yield no children, while async readdir failures can reject when
the walker reads the null result. Dangling links and links to files exercise this
behavior. Do not silently change it to promise sync/async error parity.

Matcher adaptations: use a neutral POSIX default instead of inspecting process
or environment globals; retain Node 22's literal whole-portion empty extglobs
(?(), *(), +(), @(), including empty alternatives). All remaining parsing,
nonrecursive matching and bounded matcher caching come from upstream.

